---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/home/Hero.astro';
import HomeSection from '../components/home/HomeSection.astro';
import SameReadout from '../components/home/SameReadout.astro';
import FourSteps from '../components/home/FourSteps.astro';
import PlanTable from '../components/home/PlanTable.astro';
import Subjects from '../components/home/Subjects.astro';
import Evidence from '../components/home/Evidence.astro';
import Limits from '../components/home/Limits.astro';
import TryIt from '../components/home/TryIt.astro';
import { RUN } from '../data/crosstalk';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'DeepCausality Quantum | Find the cause of correlated qubit errors';
const description =
  'A Rust library for choosing between rival causes of quantum errors. Declare the candidate causal structures; it screens them, plans the cheapest experiments that tell them apart, and names the one the data leaves standing.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'deep_causality_quantum',
  description,
  programmingLanguage: 'Rust',
  codeRepository: GITHUB_URL,
  license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero />

  <HomeSection ix="02 / 08" label="The problem" title="Passive data cannot tell a direct cause from a shared one.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Two qubits show correlated error events. One may drive the other, or both may answer to a
        shared source of noise, and the repair differs with the cause. Over two yes-or-no error
        indicators, all three structures reproduce any observed pattern exactly. Watching harder
        does not help.
      </p>
      <p>
        An intervention separates them. Hold qubit 1 excited and read qubit 2: H1 predicts 0.40,
        and H2 and H3 predict 0.10. Which experiments to run is a calculation, and QCL does it.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <SameReadout />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="03 / 08" label="How it works" title="Four steps in one program.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        You write the problem as one Rust program that declares, screens, plans and decides. Each
        card maps onto a call in the code beneath it, in the same order.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <FourSteps />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="04 / 08" label="The plan" title="Two experiments separate all three.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        The planner solves a minimum-cost cover. Some chosen experiment must separate each pair
        of candidates by at least {RUN.floorBits} bits, measured at that experiment’s shot count,
        and the plan lists any pair that no experiment reaches.
        The passive run separates nothing. Tomography separates everything for {RUN.tomographyCost}.
        Holding qubit 1 excited resolves two of the three pairs, holding qubit 2 excited resolves
        two, and the two experiments together, at cost 1 each, cover all three.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <PlanTable />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="05 / 08" label="Other subjects" title="The same builder takes models, codes and stacked abstractions.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        The subject you name after <code>config</code> decides which checks exist. A plant has
        candidates and experiments, a quantum causal model has factors that must commute, and a
        code has structural checks that run exactly. These excerpts come from three more
        examples.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Subjects />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="06 / 08" label="Evidence" title="What has been checked.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Each row names its source, so you can rerun it. The counts are the figures the tools
        report.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Evidence />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="07 / 08" label="Limits" title="What it does not do.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>These are the limits a new user meets first.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Limits />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection id="run" ix="08 / 08" label="Run it" title="Run it yourself.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>One command runs the crosstalk example from a clean clone.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <TryIt />
  </HomeSection>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
</BaseLayout>
